From 01c0dbd5ee5dcf504f3a7745244469f4ee8e82ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fozzfut <vanolia13@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 19:07:17 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] Gui: Python document observers do not see a view provider
 under construction (issue 040)

A document view provider is attached to its object right after construction
(Gui::Document::slotNewObject). Before that, base-class constructors already set
properties (ViewProviderGeometryObject::Selectable) and the change is signalled.
DocumentObserverPython wrapped the view provider at that moment: getPyObject()
of the base class under construction created and cached the base wrapper
(ViewProviderGeometryObjectPy) for the life of the view provider, so
ViewProviderAssembly, ViewProviderPartExt, PartDesign, Mesh and FEM view providers
lost their own Python API, and ViewProviderFemMesh could not be created
("Cannot add static property") as soon as any Python Gui observer with
slotChangedObject/slotBeforeChangeObject was registered.

slotBeforeChangeObject/slotChangedObject now skip a document view provider that is
not attached yet (getObject() == nullptr).

Declared change: Python Gui observers no longer receive the property changes a
view provider makes before ViewProviderDocumentObject::attach() sets its object:
(1) the default values set by constructors, and (2) the part of a derived attach()
that runs before it calls the base attach(). Of the 52 attach() overrides only
ViewProviderSubShapeBinder::attach has such a part (ViewProviderShapeBinder.cpp:
UseBinderStyle.setValue and its onChanged cascade ShapeAppearance, LineColor,
LineColorArray, PointColor, PointColorArray, PointMaterial, Transparency,
LineWidth). Stock handed those events to Python with Object == None. Every event
after the object is set is unchanged; C++ subscribers are untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/Gui/DocumentObserverPython.cpp | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/Gui/DocumentObserverPython.cpp b/src/Gui/DocumentObserverPython.cpp
index 426aed9..77ca320 100644
--- a/src/Gui/DocumentObserverPython.cpp
+++ b/src/Gui/DocumentObserverPython.cpp
@@ -34,6 +34,26 @@ namespace sp = std::placeholders;
 
 std::vector<DocumentObserverPython*> DocumentObserverPython::_instances;
 
+namespace
+{
+// A document view provider is attached to its object right after it is constructed
+// (Gui::Document::slotNewObject: createInstance() then attach()). Before that it is still being
+// constructed: base-class constructors already set properties (e.g. Selectable in
+// ViewProviderGeometryObject), and every such change is signalled. Handing the view provider to
+// Python at that point calls getPyObject() while only a base class is constructed, so the base
+// class's wrapper (e.g. ViewProviderGeometryObjectPy) is created and cached in pyViewObject for the
+// whole life of the view provider: its own Python API (ViewProviderAssembly.isInEditMode,
+// ViewProviderPartExt, PartDesign ViewProvider, Mesh, FEM ...) is lost. The property-name lookup
+// for the observer also merges the property table of the class under construction, so a
+// constructor that adds a property after such a signal throws "Cannot add static property"
+// (ViewProviderFemMesh). Python observers therefore see a view provider only once it is attached.
+bool isUnderConstruction(const Gui::ViewProvider& vp)
+{
+    return vp.isDerivedFrom<ViewProviderDocumentObject>()
+        && !static_cast<const ViewProviderDocumentObject&>(vp).getObject();
+}
+}  // namespace
+
 void DocumentObserverPython::addObserver(const Py::Object& obj)
 {
     _instances.push_back(new DocumentObserverPython(obj));
@@ -196,6 +216,9 @@ void DocumentObserverPython::slotBeforeChangeObject(
     const App::Property& Prop
 )
 {
+    if (isUnderConstruction(Obj)) {
+        return;
+    }
     Base::PyGILStateLocker lock;
     try {
         Py::Tuple args(2);
@@ -216,6 +239,9 @@ void DocumentObserverPython::slotBeforeChangeObject(
 
 void DocumentObserverPython::slotChangedObject(const Gui::ViewProvider& Obj, const App::Property& Prop)
 {
+    if (isUnderConstruction(Obj)) {
+        return;
+    }
     Base::PyGILStateLocker lock;
     try {
         Py::Tuple args(2);
